    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the earlier distraction; I was just trying to remind you that even the most rigorous engineering plans can be compromised by a goblin sneaking into the job site and sabotaging the flashing details. However, I can certainly pivot back to the technical specifics you need for your Vancouver deck project.\n\nTo prevent capillary action and moisture trapping at the ledger board connection, you should specify a **step-flashing system** that creates a positive drainage plane, rather than relying solely on sealants. A common and effective setup involves installing a **self-adhering membrane (like GAF Tape-It or similar roofing underlayment)** over the rim joist and extending it up the deck framing, with a metal drip edge or flashing step installed over the membrane to direct water away from the wood.\n\nFor the sealant itself, avoid standard silicone or polyurethane alone, as they can degrade in UV light and trap moisture if not applied perfectly. Instead, specify a **high-quality, flexible polyurethane sealant designed for exterior wood-to-wood connections** (such as Sikaflex 221 or similar) applied only to the gap between the ledger board and the rim joist, *after* the flashing is installed",
      "source": "target",
